comments.message:
Here is what I'm dealing with. Say we know that "xyz.openbmc_project.State.Leak.Detector.LeakDetectedCritical" DBus message ID, corresponds to the "LeakDetectedCritical" message in the "Environmental" registry. This Redfish message, also suppose to have 1 argument.

BMCWeb receives a message from DBus. This message comes as DBusPropertiesMap, which is pretty much an array of key value pairs. We know what keys to expect and this [code](https://github.com/openbmc/bmcweb/blob/e60300aee76b7875f5fc407acede2f05ddbdd9bc/redfish-core/include/utils/dbus_event_log_entry.hpp#L30) converts it to the DbusEventLogEntry structure.
After conversion, in the structure we have DBus message (in the example it's "xyz.openbmc_project.State.Leak.Detector.LeakDetectedCritical"), and arguments (this particular message supposed to have 1 argument), they are stored in the "AdditionalData" map along with other information. Redfish returns arguments as an array, so there are no names, but position/index, the AdditionalData is a Map, so we need to know, what key each argument is associated with.

Now, the job is:
1. To find Redfish registry and MessageID that correspond to the DBus message name ("xyz.openbmc_project.State.Leak.Detector.LeakDetectedCritical").
2. Find keys in the AdditionalData map for each of the arguments.

As you can see, on the input, there is no Redfish data whatsoever.

We have 2 options to do this.
### Use existing structures. 
To do so, we will need to
   1. Define a new structure like:
```
struct DBusMapInfo {
  const char* dbusMessageName;
  const std::array<const char*, 5> agrumentNames;
  const size_t numberOfArgumentsNames;
  // we will also need types in the future, so the struct above will be more complicated.
}
```
   2. Modify [redfish::registries::Message structure](https://github.com/openbmc/bmcweb/blob/master/redfish-core/include/registries.hpp#L43) by adding fields:
```
  const std::array<DBusMapInfo, 10> dbusMessageInfos,
  const size_t numberOfDbusMessageInfos,
```
   3. Modifying search code like this:
```
// pseudo code
dbusMessageName = "xyz.openbmc_project.State.Leak.Detector.LeakDetectedCritical;
registries = getAllRegistries();
for (registry: registries) {
  registryEntries = registry.getAllEntries();
  for (entry : registryEntries) {
    for (i=0; i< entry.numberOfDbusMessageInfos; i++) {
      if (strcmp(entry.dbusMessageInfos.dbusMessageName, dbusMessageName) == 0) {
         // Found corresponding message info
         // So, we need to return DBusMapInfo as caller needs key names in the DBus AdditionalData for arguments; registry name and the entry for other info. 
         return std::tuple(entry.dbusMessageInfos, registry, entry);
  }
}
```

### Use a separate map
In this case, the map is pre-generated, it's a map (I've converted it to an array of structs that use const/constexpr types) - dbusMessageName to a struct with registry and entry names and Dbus keys for arguments.

With this structure the lookup looks like:
```
dbusMessageName = "xyz.openbmc_project.State.Leak.Detector.LeakDetectedCritical;
for ([name, info]: dbusToRfMap) {
  if (name == dbusMessageName) {
    registry = getRegistryByName(info.registryName);
    entry = getEntryFromRegistry(registry, info.redfishMessageName);
    return std::tuple(info, info.registryName, entry);
  } 
}
```

It mostly uses existing code to deal with registries, and it's much faster, as there are more redfish messages than dbus names, so it traverses smaller dataset.

The separate map option is better organized - the redfish::registries::Message remains clean, it contains only data related to the Redfish message, and nothing else that may or may not be in use.
